`GAMPIL PROGRAMMING LANGUAGE`


bitOff main[asc8()() args]:
    
    printf ["Hello World!"]		\ 'Hello World!' program
 
    rat32 y be 0.5			\ variable uses 'be' to initialize
    num16 x; x be 3			\ use ';' for one line
    num16 z be 4

    printf ["added: %d", add[z,b be x]]	\ calling declared function
    printf ["added: %d", add[z, ]]	\ pre-initialized variable can be skipped

    num16(3) num be {1, 3, 5}		\ table row type for same type only
    num32() num2 be num32()[malloc[2]]	\ if the size is empty, variable becomes pointer
    num16() num3 be @x			\ it can also point a variable
    field user be {1, "John Doe"}	\ table column type, with different types are valid as element

    field hash be {num16 id be 2, asc8() name be "John Doe", 6, 7, "hello"}	
    \ hash field type, like lua tables but adding types

    printf ["%d", num(0)]		\ table index starts from 0
    printf ["%d", user(0)]		\ either column table
    printf ["%d", hash.id]		\ except the hashed one

    \ if statements
    if x > 5:
           printn ["Higher than 5"]	\ first condition, use 'but' to end block
         but
       x = 5:
           printn ["Equal to 5"]	\ second condition
         but
       x <= 5:
           printn ["Less than 5"]	\ if both second and third true, execution would be parallel
         but				\ these are called 'parallel guard branching'

    `
       parallel guard branching is inspired 
       by Djikstra's Guard Command Language 
    `

       else and x >= 5:
           printn ["Exclusion found!"]	\ use 'else' followed by 'and' keyword to prevent these 
           \ you can also place 'else' everywhere, like "x >= 5 and else" or even only "else" if there are no other conditions
         but
      ok

     ` need loops! we invented 'redo' loop! `

    redo num as num16 i:
        printn ["Looping"]		\ looping exact 3 times (only static size tables or python runtime tables are valid, malloc ect. not included)
      ok

    redo num quite as num16 j:
        printn ["Looping"]		\ looping 3 times
        stop				\ but it can stops 
      ok

    redo:
        if not x >= 0: 			\ if there is only 'redo' with no 'stop', it loops forever 
            stop			\ there is a 'stop' keyword, so it only loops until "not x >= 0" is true
          but ok
        x -be 1				\ decrementing x
      ok

    ` 
       the block 'redo: if not x >= 0: stop; but ok ...' can be replaced by
       'redo: while x >= 0; ...' for more conveinence.
    `

    redo: while x < 5			\ preprocessor will convert it into 'redo: if not x < 5: stop; but ok ...'
        x be x + 1
      ok

    `
      loops are inspired by BlooP and FlooP
      by Douglas Hofstadter
    `
  ok					\ keyword 'ok' to end blocks

num16 add[num16 a, num16 b be 0]:
    return a + b			\ returning value to function
  ok

bitOff greet[]:
    print ["Greetings!"]		\ no return type
  ok

` portable types:

   There are bits (bitOff/bitOn), encodings (enc), numbers (num) and rational numbers (rat).

   bit<On or Off>	: length 0 (bitOff) or 1 (bitOn) bit, 0 for void type
   asc<size>		: length <size> bit
      size can be 8 to 64 exponentially, unsigned (asc8, asc16, asc32, asc64)
   num<size>		: length <size> bit
      size can be 8 to 64 exponentially, but signed (num8, num16, num32, num64)
   rat<size>		: length <size> bit
      size can be 32 to 128 exponentially (rat32, rat64, rat128)
   field		: table type (works like lua table, but sizes and types are statically inferenced)
`


       


   

